16:50:56 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I understand the shift you're making. You're saying: forget biology, forget IQ. The group-level choices—staying, adapting in certain ways, not developing faster—look impractical and short-sighted. And "dumb" is just a blunt word for choices that produce bad outcomes. Fair enough. But here's the problem with that framing. Calling a group's choices "dumb" assumes you understand the full range of options they had, the constraints they faced, and the values they were prioritizing. Most of the time, you don't. What looks like an impractical choice from the outside often makes perfect sense when you understand the internal logic. Take the relaxed pace you mentioned earlier. From a productivity-obsessed Western mindset, it looks like laziness. From a survival-in-extreme-heat mindset, it's exactly the right call. The choice isn't "dumb." It's smart. It's just optimizing for a different variable than you're used to valuing. Many choices that seem short-sighted in poor countries are actually long-term rational responses to unstable conditions. Why not invest in a business? Because the government might seize it. Why not save money? Because inflation will eat it. Why not plan for decades ahead? Because political chaos makes a five-year horizon a luxury. The apparent short-sightedness isn't a cultural defect. It's a logical adaptation to an environment where the future is highly unpredictable. Change the environment, and the long-term thinking you're looking for often appears. So you can call choices "dumb" if you want. But it usually just means "I don't understand the context that made those choices sensible." And that's not a description of them. It's a confession about your own perspective.
